(e) Plant Health and Replacement. 1. Healthy condition. All Plants shall maintain a healthy color and full, vigorous appearance appropriate to the species, recognizing that Plants undergo seasonal changes (for example, Copperleaf should show vibrant red, pink, or coppery color, and Areca palms should be predominantly green). Owners shall prune, fertilize (in compliance with applicable county and state fertilizer restrictions), and treat Plants for disease and infestation as needed to keep them healthy year-round, and shall provide regular care for everything they plant.

2. Definition of "Failing Plant." A Plant is a "Failing Plant" if it is dead, or if any of the following apply:

o (i) it has lost fifty percent (50%) or more of its normal foliage or canopy;

o (ii) it is predominantly brown, yellow, or otherwise discolored for its species;

o (iii) it has significant dead branches, bare areas, or gaps that prevent it from presenting a full and healthy appearance;

o (iv) it no longer serves its intended landscape function, including a Hedge that no longer provides the screening required by this Section; or

o (v) in the case of a Tree, it has lost thirty percent (30%) or more of its canopy or structural framework, has been reduced in height or crown by one-third (1/3) or more as a result of storm, lightning, disease, pruning, or other damage, is leaning or structurally compromised so as to pose a hazard, or no longer satisfies the tree requirements of the applicable county code.

A Plant is not healthy or compliant merely because it has some living leaves, branches, or tissue. Temporary seasonal leaf drop or dormancy of species that naturally do so is not a Failing Plant. 3. Removal and replacement. Except for Trees, which are governed by subsection (g), a Failing Plant shall be removed and replaced with a healthy Plant of comparable type and size within thirty (30) days after written notice from the Association. If the Owner demonstrates that the Plant is recovering from recent pruning, storm damage, or transplanting, the Reviewer may allow up to ninety (90) days for recovery before replacement is required.
